  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global best practices on emerging chemical policy issues of concern under the Strategic Approach to International Chemicals Management (SAICM) (EDCs, EPPPs)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humans and ecosystems are simultaneously exposed to multiple combinations of chemicals, multiplying uncertainty around impacts of exposure to individual chemicals. Many chemicals may feature in one or more categories. In this context, the chemical-by-chemical regulatory approach is increasingly unable to address the global environmental and health risks posed by these chemicals. Activities under this project therefore aim to contribute to an integrated, collaborative and multistakeholder approach needed, being executed under SAICM.     Through activities on the emerging pollutants, EPPPs and EDCs, this project is expected to produce data points and increase scientific capacity in LMICs. This evidence is expected to drive adoption of policies, for example on disposal of medicines, on handling and treatment of pharmaceutical wastes from healthcare facilities, and on controlling hazardous discharges in effluent from manufacturing facilities. Such policies will lead to socio-economic benefits through reduced contamination of water and soil resources. Improved and demonstrable environmental management of manufacturing facilities is essential for the long-term development of such production in LMICs, and the project will help to ensure that the shift in production of pharmaceuticals from developed to developing countries is not accompanied by unacceptable environmental problems.                       Normal   0               false   false   false      EN-US   ZH-CN   AR-SA                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 